1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,620
Today, we'll look at a line of code together.

2
00:00:03,151 --> 00:00:09,521
Before Swift arrived, developers were already building iPhone apps in a programming language called Objective-C.

3
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:14,396
Swift could use Apple’s interface toolkit, UIKit, for the same drawing operations.

4
00:00:14,945 --> 00:00:20,865
For this call in Swift, the proposal aimed to remove repeated words and make the destination clear.

5
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:25,750
The second line moves the path’s current point to the point we just created.

6
00:00:26,269 --> 00:00:29,119
We can drop one word from the name on the second line and still understand the call.

7
00:00:30,299 --> 00:00:32,219
The first line already shows the point’s type.

8
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:37,244
In the December draft, only Point disappeared. To still ended the method name.

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,250
In Swift 3, the method name ends with move.

10
00:00:41,788 --> 00:00:43,608
The word to now comes immediately before the destination.

11
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:47,110
Placing the pencil at the starting point leaves the paper unmarked.

12
00:00:47,602 --> 00:00:50,822
Connect the corners and stroke the path, and a paper airplane appears.

13
00:00:51,344 --> 00:00:55,514
Written this way, the call shows where to move without repeating “point”.
